1. Number of Threads (Users)

Meaning:
Total virtual users JMeter will simulate.

How it works:
Each thread = 1 virtual user sending requests.

Example:

50 threads → 50 users hitting your system

How to choose:

Based on expected load:
Small test: 10–50 users
Medium: 100–500 users
Heavy: 1000+ users
Start small, then increase gradually (important for finding bottlenecks)
🔹 2. Ramp-Up Period (seconds)

Meaning:
Time JMeter takes to start all threads.

How it works:
Threads are started evenly over this time.

Formula:

Delay between each thread = Ramp-up / Number of threads

Example:

Threads = 10
Ramp-up = 20 seconds
→ 1 thread starts every 2 seconds

Why it matters:

Prevents sudden spike
Simulates real-world gradual user arrival

How to choose:

Realistic load → keep ramp-up ≈ number of threads
Stress test → smaller ramp-up (faster load spike)
Spike test → ramp-up = 0 or very low
🔹 3. Loop Count

Meaning:
How many times each thread repeats the test.

Options:

Fixed number (e.g., 5)
Infinite (checkbox selected)

How it works:

Loop = how many iterations per user

Example:

10 threads × loop 5 → 50 total executions

Infinite loop:

Runs until manually stopped or duration controller is used

How to choose:

Short test → fixed loops
Long-running test → infinite + duration control
🔹 4. Same User on Each Iteration

Meaning:
Controls whether JMeter resets session/cookies between loops.

If checked:

Same user session reused
Cookies, cache preserved
Simulates real returning user

If unchecked:

Each loop = new fresh user
Session reset

How to choose:

Login/session-based apps → ✔ enable
Stateless APIs → ❌ disable
🔹 5. Delay Thread Creation Until Needed

Meaning:
Threads are created only when required, not all at once.

Why useful:

Saves memory
Helps with large-scale tests

How it works:

Threads are initialized gradually instead of upfront

How to choose:

Large load (1000+ users) → ✔ enable
Small tests → not necessary
🔹 6. Specify Thread Lifetime

When enabled, you get:

➤ Duration (seconds)

Total time the test will run

➤ Startup Delay (seconds)

Wait time before test starts

How it works:

Threads run until duration ends (even if loop not finished)

Example:

Duration = 60 sec
→ test runs for 1 minute
🔥 Putting It All Together (Example)
Scenario: 100 users over 5 minutes
Threads = 100
Ramp-up = 100 seconds
Loop = Infinite
Duration = 300 seconds
Same user = ✔
Delay thread creation = ✔

👉 Result:

Users gradually start
Test runs for 5 minutes
Realistic user behavior



Startup Delay (seconds)

👉 What it means:
Time JMeter waits before starting any threads at all

👉 How it works:

Test is launched
JMeter does nothing for the delay time
After delay → threads start as per ramp-up

Example:

Startup Delay = 10 sec
Threads = 5
Ramp-up = 5 sec

👉 Timeline:

0–10 sec → nothing happens
After 10 sec → threads start (1 per second)

👉 Use cases:

Wait for server/app to be ready
Synchronize with external events
Stagger multiple test runs
🔹 2. Delay Thread Creation Until Needed

👉 What it means:
Controls WHEN threads are created in memory, not when they start running.

👉 How it works:

❌ If NOT checked (default)
All threads are created immediately at test start
Even if they start later (due to ramp-up)
✔ If checked
Threads are created gradually as needed
Matches ramp-up timing

Example:

Threads = 1000
Ramp-up = 1000 sec

👉 Without delay option:

1000 threads created instantly → high memory usage

👉 With delay option:

1 thread created per second → efficient


Authorization / Token

Postman: You set Bearer tokens, Basic Auth, OAuth, etc.

JMeter equivalent:

HTTP Header Manager – add Authorization: Bearer <token> header.
HTTP Authorization Manager – supports Basic, Digest, NTLM authentication automatically.
Dynamic tokens:
Use JSON Extractor to extract a token from a login response
Store in a JMeter Variable (e.g., ${authToken})
Use it in headers for subsequent requests

Example:

Login request → JSON Extractor → save token as authToken
Header Manager → Authorization: Bearer ${authToken}
2️⃣ Headers

Postman: You can add any HTTP header per request.

JMeter equivalent:

HTTP Header Manager – attach to HTTP Request sampler.
Can be global (attach at Thread Group level) or per-request.
3️⃣ Variables / Environment

Postman: Variables store reusable data like URL, user ID, tokens.

JMeter equivalent:

User Defined Variables – set global variables per test plan
CSV Data Set Config – data-driven variables (more below)
JMeter Variables from extractors – dynamically get data from responses
4️⃣ Scripting / Tests

Postman: Pre-request scripts or test scripts (JS code).

JMeter equivalent:

JSR223 Sampler / PreProcessor / PostProcessor – write Groovy (faster than Beanshell)
Use for:
Dynamic data manipulation
Custom assertions
Generating random values

Example: Generate random user ID:

vars.put("userId", UUID.randomUUID().toString())
5️⃣ Extract Data from JSON Response

Postman: Use pm.response.json()

JMeter equivalent:

JSON Extractor
Steps:
