Postman: Tests like pm.expect(response.code).to.equal(200)

JMeter equivalent:

Response Assertion – check text, code, or JSON
JSR223 Assertion – custom validation using Groovy


🔹 What is Constant Timer?

A Constant Timer adds a fixed delay (pause) before every sampler (request) is executed.

👉 Think of it like:
“Wait X milliseconds before sending each request.”

🔹 How to Use Constant Timer
Step-by-step:
Open your Test Plan in Apache JMeter
Right-click on your Thread Group
Go to → Add → Timer → Constant Timer
In the Constant Timer settings:
Thread Delay (in milliseconds) → enter the delay time
🔹 Example

If you set:

Thread Delay = 2000 ms

👉 Behavior:

JMeter will wait 2 seconds before every request
🔹 How It Behaves
1. Applied to Each Sampler
The delay is applied before each request

If you have multiple samplers:

Request 1 → wait → execute
Request 2 → wait → execute
2. Per Thread (User)
Each virtual user (thread) follows the delay independently

Example:

5 users
2-second delay

👉 Each user waits 2 seconds before their own requests

3. Total Execution Flow

Without timer:

Request → Request → Request (very fast)

With Constant Timer (2000 ms):

Wait 2s → Request → Wait 2s → Request → Wait 2s → Request
4. Important Note ⚠️
The delay is added in addition to response time

Example:

Server response = 500 ms
Timer = 2000 ms

👉 Total time per request ≈ 2.5 seconds

🔹 Where to Add Timer Matters
Under Thread Group → applies to all requests
Under specific sampler → applies only to that request
Under controller → applies to requests inside that controller

🔹 When to Use Constant Timer

Use it when you want:

Fixed user think time
Simple load pacing
Avoid hitting server too aggressively


🔹 What is Uniform Random Timer?

It adds a random delay before each request, within a defined range.

👉 Formula used:

Delay = Constant Delay Offset + Random Delay (0 to Maximum Random Delay)
🔹 How to Use It
Steps:
Open your Test Plan in Apache JMeter
Right-click on Thread Group (or sampler)
Go to → Add → Timer → Uniform Random Timer
Configure:
Random Delay Maximum (ms) → upper bound of randomness
Constant Delay Offset (ms) → minimum delay
🔹 Example
Input:
Random Delay Maximum = 3000 ms
Constant Delay Offset = 2000 ms
Output Behavior:

Each request delay will be:

2000 ms + (0 to 3000 ms random)

👉 So actual delay range:
2000 ms to 5000 ms

🔹 How It Behaves
1. Delay Before Each Request

Just like Constant Timer:

Wait → Request → Wait → Request

But now:

Wait time is different every time
2. Per Thread (User)

Each virtual user gets its own random delay

Example:

User 1 → waits 2.3 sec
User 2 → waits 4.7 sec
User 3 → waits 3.1 sec
3. Execution Flow Example
Random wait (2–5s) → Request  
Random wait (2–5s) → Request  
Random wait (2–5s) → Request
4. Adds to Response Time ⚠️

If:

Random delay = 3000 ms
Response time = 500 ms

👉 Total ≈ 3.5 seconds

🔹 Where to Place It
Thread Group → applies to all requests
Controller → applies to requests inside it
Sampler → applies only to that request
🔹 Why Use Uniform Random Timer?

✅ Simulates real user behavior
(real users don’t click at exact intervals)

✅ Avoids traffic spikes
(prevents all users hitting server at same time)

✅ Makes test more realistic

What is Precise Throughput Timer?

It ensures that requests are sent at a specific target rate (throughput) over time.

👉 Unlike other timers:

It focuses on requests per time unit
Not just adding delays
🔹 How to Use It
Steps:
Open your Test Plan in JMeter
Right-click → Thread Group
Go to → Add → Timer → Precise Throughput Timer
Configure these fields:
Important Fields:
Target Throughput (samples per period)
Throughput Period (seconds)

👉 Together they define:

Throughput = Requests / Time
🔹 Example
Input:
Target Throughput = 60
Period = 60 seconds

👉 Result:

1 request per second
Another Example:
Target Throughput = 120
Period = 60 seconds

👉 Result:

2 requests per second
🔹 How It Behaves
1. Smooth Request Distribution

Instead of bursts, it spreads requests evenly:

Without timer:

Burst → many requests at once → idle → burst

With Precise Throughput Timer:

Request → wait → request → wait → request (even spacing)
2. Dynamic Waiting Time
JMeter calculates delay automatically
Delay changes depending on:
Current throughput
Target throughput

👉 You don’t set delay manually

3. Across All Threads
Works globally across threads
Coordinates users to maintain overall rate

Example:

10 users
Target = 10 req/sec

👉 Each user may send ~1 request/sec (adjusted dynamically)

4. If System is Slow ⚠️

If response time is high:

Timer cannot exceed system capacity

Example:

Target = 100 req/sec
Server can handle only 50

👉 Actual throughput ≈ 50 req/sec

1. Test Duration (seconds)
✅ What it is:
Total time for which the timer will plan and distribute requests
🔧 How it behaves:
JMeter spreads requests evenly across this duration
📌 Example:
Throughput = 600 requests
Test Duration = 60 sec

👉 JMeter will aim:

600 requests / 60 sec = 10 requests per second
⚠️ Important:
If your Thread Group runs longer than this → timer may stop controlling properly after duration
Best practice:
👉 Match Test Duration with Thread Group duration
🔹 2. Number of Threads in Batch
✅ What it is:
Number of threads (users) that will be released together in one group (batch)
🔧 How it behaves:
Threads are not all activated at once
They are released in small groups
📌 Example:
Total Threads = 50
Threads in Batch = 10

👉 Execution:

First 10 threads start  
Next 10 threads start  
Next 10 threads start...
🎯 Why this matters:
Prevents sudden spikes
Helps smoother traffic distribution
🔹 3. Delay Between Threads in Batch (ms)
✅ What it is:
Time gap between starting each batch
🔧 How it behaves:
📌 Example:
Threads in Batch = 10
Delay between batches = 2000 ms

👉 Execution:

Start 10 threads  
(wait 2 sec)  
Start next 10 threads  
(wait 2 sec)  
Start next 10 threads
🎯 Purpose:
Avoids all users starting at same time
Helps gradual ramp-up (alternative to ramp-up in Thread Group)
🔹 4. Random Seed
✅ What it is:
Controls the randomization logic used internally by the timer
🔧 How it behaves:
Case 1: Same seed value
JMeter generates same request pattern every run

👉 Good for:

Reproducible tests
Debugging
Case 2: Different / empty seed
JMeter generates different pattern every time

👉 Good for:

Real-world simulation
📌 Example:
Seed = 123 → same timing pattern every run
Seed empty → new random distribution each run
🔹 Putting It All Together (Real Example)
Configuration:
Target Throughput = 600 requests
Test Duration = 60 sec
Threads = 50
Threads in Batch = 10
Delay between batch = 2 sec
Behavior:
Timer plans → 10 requests/sec
Threads start in groups of 10 every 2 seconds
Requests are evenly distributed across 60 sec
If seed is fixed → same pattern each run
🔹 Important Observations ⚠️
1. Timer controls rate, not thread creation
Thread Group still controls total users
Timer only controls when requests fire
2. Batch settings affect smoothness
Smaller batches → smoother traffic
Larger batches → bursty traffic



🔹 What is Constant Throughput Timer?

It tries to ensure your test runs at a target throughput (requests per minute) by adding delays dynamically.

👉 Key idea:

“Adjust wait time so total requests ≈ target throughput”
🔹 How to Use It
Steps:
Add → Timer → Constant Throughput Timer
Configure:
Important Fields:
1. Target Throughput
Measured in samples per minute

👉 Example:

600 → means 600 requests/min
= 10 requests/sec
2. Calculate Throughput Based On

This is the most important setting ⚠️
It controls how throughput is shared.

Options:

🔸 1. This thread only

👉 Each user tries to achieve the target independently

Example:
Threads = 10
Throughput = 60 req/min

👉 Result:

Each thread → 60 req/min  
Total → 600 req/min
🔸 2. All active threads (shared)

👉 All users together achieve the target

Example:
Threads = 10
Throughput = 600 req/min

👉 Result:

Total → 600 req/min  
Each thread ≈ 60 req/min
🔸 3. All active threads in current thread group

👉 Same as above, but limited to that thread group

🔸 4. All active threads (shared) in all thread groups

👉 Across entire test plan

🔹 How It Behaves
1. Works by Adding Delays
JMeter calculates how fast requests are going
If too fast → adds delay
If too slow → no delay
2. Not Perfectly Smooth ⚠️
Can create small bursts
Less accurate than Precise Throughput Timer
3. Depends on Threads
Needs enough users to maintain rate
4. Affected by Response Time

Example:

Target = 600 req/min
Response time = 2 sec

👉 You’ll need more threads to keep rate

🔹 Example Scenario
Config:
Threads = 10
Target Throughput = 600 req/min
Mode = All active threads
Behavior:
JMeter ensures:
Total ≈ 10 requests/sec
Adds delays dynamically between requests
🔹 Constant vs Precise Throughput Timer
Feature	Constant Throughput Timer	Precise Throughput Timer
Accuracy	Medium	High
Distribution	Can be uneven	Very smooth
Control	Delay-based	Schedule-based
Complexity	Simple	Advanced
🔹 When to Use Constant Throughput Timer

✅ Simple throughput control
✅ Basic API testing
✅ When slight inaccuracy is okay

🔹 When NOT to Use

❌ Need exact RPS
❌ Need perfectly smooth traffic
❌ High-precision performance testing

👉 Use Precise Throughput Timer instead

🔹 Common Mistakes ⚠️
❌ Wrong mode selection
“This thread only” → multiplies throughput unexpectedly
❌ Too few threads
Can’t reach target throughput
❌ Ignoring units
It’s requests per minute, not per second

