🚀 What is Throughput?
📌 Definition

Throughput = Number of requests processed per unit time

It is usually expressed as:

requests/second (req/sec)
requests/minute (req/min)
🧠 Simple analogy

Think of a toll booth:

Cars = API requests
Booth = server/API
Throughput = how many cars pass per second

👉 Higher throughput = more traffic handled efficiently

📊 In API testing context

If you run a load test:

1000 requests sent in 10 seconds
and 800 succeed in that time

👉 Throughput ≈ 80 req/sec

⚙️ How JMeter calculates it

In Apache JMeter:

Based on completed requests
Divided by total test duration

So:

Throughput = (Total requests completed) / (Total time taken)
📈 Where you see it

You’ll see throughput in:

Summary Report
Aggregate Report
HTML Dashboard Report
🔍 How to interpret Throughput
🟢 High throughput

✔ System handles more requests per second
✔ Good scalability
✔ Efficient backend

🔴 Low throughput

❌ System processes fewer requests
❌ Possible bottlenecks:

slow DB
thread exhaustion
network latency
⚠️ Important insights (very important in interviews)
1. Throughput ≠ Response Time
Response time = how fast one request completes
Throughput = how many requests system can handle overall

👉 You can have:

Low response time but low throughput (limited concurrency)
High throughput but slightly higher response time (balanced load system)


What is Latency?

👉 Latency = Time taken for the server to start responding after a request is sent

It does NOT wait for the full response to complete.

🧠 Simple analogy

Think of ordering food at a restaurant:

You place an order (request sent)
Wait time until chef starts serving your food = latency
Time until you finish eating = full response time
📊 In API testing terms

When you call an API:

Request is sent
Server receives it
Server processes it
First byte of response is received

👉 Time from (1 → 4) = Latency

⚙️ Latency in JMeter

In Apache JMeter:

Latency is the time until first byte of response arrives
It is shown in:
Aggregate Report
View Results Tree
📌 Latency vs Response Time (VERY IMPORTANT)
Metric	Meaning
Latency	Time until first byte of response
Response Time	Total time to get full response
📈 Example

Let’s say:

Request sent at: 0 ms
First byte received: 200 ms → Latency = 200 ms
Full response received: 500 ms → Response time = 500 ms


