Throughput Controller – Fields & Behavior

When you add a Throughput Controller, you will see these main options:

1️⃣ Throughput (Value field)
📌 What it means

This is the main control value.

It defines:

Percentage of execution OR
Number of executions (depending on mode)
2️⃣ Calculate Throughput based on

This is the MOST important setting.

It has 2 modes:

🔹 A) Percent Executions (most used)
📌 Meaning:

Value is treated as percentage

Example:

50 → means 50% of iterations
20 → means 20% of iterations
⚙️ How it behaves

Assume:

100 users
Throughput = 50%

👉 JMeter will:

Randomly allow ~50 users
Skip ~50 users

✔ Not exact, but approximately distributed

🧠 Key behavior:
Works like probability
Each iteration is independent
🔹 B) Total Executions
📌 Meaning:

Value is treated as fixed count of executions

Example:

10 → run only 10 times total (across all threads)
⚙️ How it behaves

Assume:

100 users
Throughput = 10 executions

👉 Result:

Only first 10 eligible executions will run
Remaining users skip it

✔ This is GLOBAL LIMIT mode

3️⃣ Per User (checkbox in some versions)
📌 Meaning:

Controls whether limit applies:

per thread (user)
or globally
🔹 If enabled (Per User = true)

Each user gets its own quota.

Example:

Throughput = 2 executions
5 users

👉 Each user runs it 2 times:

Total = 10 executions
🔹 If disabled (Per User = false)

Quota is shared across all users.

Example:

Throughput = 2 executions
5 users

👉 Only 2 executions total (shared)

🧪 Your Setup
Based on → Total Executions
Throughput value → 10
Per User → CHECKED
Users → 100 threads
🎯 What this means
✔ “Total Executions = 10”

👉 There is a limit of 10 executions

✔ “Per User = ON”

👉 That limit applies to each user separately

⚙️ How JMeter behaves

Each of the 100 users gets its own counter.

So:

User 1 → can execute 10 times
User 2 → can execute 10 times
User 3 → can execute 10 times
...
User 100 → can execute 10 times
📊 Final result
Users	Executions per user	Total executions
100	10	100 × 10 = 1000
🧠 Step-by-step behavior

For each user:

IF executions < 10
   run sampler
ELSE
   skip

This reset happens for every thread separately

🧪 Simple example
User 1:
Run 10 times ✔
Then stop
User 2:
Run 10 times ✔
Then stop

👉 Same behavior for all 100 users

⚠️ Important insight

This is NOT:
❌ 10 executions total for whole test

It IS:
✔ 10 executions per user

🧠 Easy way to remember
Per User ON = each user gets quota
Total Executions = size of quota

So:

100 users × 10 executions each = 1000 total executions

🚀 One-line interview answer

In JMeter, when Throughput Controller is set to Total Executions = 10 with Per User enabled and 100 users, each user independently executes the sampler 10 times, resulting in a total of 1000 executions.


Your Setup
Based on → Total Executions
Throughput value → 10
Per User → ❌ UNCHECKED
Users → 100 threads
🎯 What this means
✔ “Total Executions = 10”

👉 Only 10 executions are allowed in total

✔ “Per User = OFF”

👉 That limit is shared across all users

⚙️ How JMeter behaves

Now all 100 users compete for the same 10 executions

👉 Once 10 executions are completed:

Controller stops executing for everyone
📊 Final result
Users	Total executions allowed	Actual execution
100	10 (shared)	only 10 users execute once
🧠 Step-by-step behavior

JMeter does this internally:

global_counter = 10

for each user request:
    if global_counter > 0:
        execute sampler
        global_counter--
    else:
        skip
🧪 Example flow
Execution order	User	Result
1	User 1	runs ✔
2	User 2	runs ✔
3	User 3	runs ✔
...	...	...
10	User 10	runs ✔
11–100	remaining users	❌ skipped
🧠 Final outcome

👉 Only 10 total executions across all 100 users

⚠️ Key insight
With Per User = ON → each user gets quota
With Per User = OFF → all users share one quota



What is Interleave Controller?

👉 It makes JMeter execute only one child request per iteration, and it rotates through all child requests in sequence.

🧠 Simple idea

If you have:

Request A
Request B
Request C

👉 Interleave Controller will run like:

Iteration	Executed Request
1	A
2	B
3	C
4	A
5	B
6	C

✔ It “cycles” through requests

⚙️ How to use Interleave Controller
Structure:
Thread Group
  └── Interleave Controller
        ├── Request A
        ├── Request B
        ├── Request C
📋 Fields in Interleave Controller
1️⃣ Interleave across threads (checkbox)
📌 Meaning:

Controls whether rotation is:

Per thread OR
Shared across all threads
🔹 If NOT checked (default)

👉 Rotation happens per thread

Each user has its own sequence:

User 1: A → B → C → A
User 2: A → B → C → A

✔ Independent rotation per user

🔹 If checked

👉 Rotation is global across all users

Example with 2 users:

Step	User	Request
1	User1	A
2	User2	B
3	User1	C
4	User2	A

✔ Shared sequence across threads

🧪 Example behavior
Setup:
1 thread group
3 requests inside Interleave Controller
2 loops
Result (default behavior):
Loop 1:
  A
  B
  C

Loop 2:
  A
  B
  C

👉 Actually each iteration picks only ONE request:

Correct Interleave behavior:

Iteration	Output
1	A
2	B
3	C
4	A
5	B
6	C
📌 Key behavior points

✔ Executes only ONE child per iteration
✔ Rotates sequentially
✔ Maintains order
✔ Can be per-thread or global

Interleave Controller (Fields)

You see 2 checkboxes:

1️⃣ Ignore Sub-Controller Blocks
2️⃣ Interleave Across Threads
🧠 1️⃣ Ignore Sub-Controller Blocks
📌 Meaning

👉 JMeter will ignore nested controllers and treat only direct samplers

⚙️ Behavior
🔹 If NOT checked (default)

If you have:

Interleave Controller
   ├── Request A
   ├── Simple Controller
   │      ├── Request B
   │      └── Request C

👉 JMeter treats Simple Controller as one block

So execution rotates like:

A → (B+C together) → A → (B+C)
🔹 If CHECKED

👉 JMeter ignores grouping inside sub-controllers

So it treats everything like:

A
B
C

✔ Flat structure rotation

🧠 Simple meaning
Option	Behavior
Unchecked	respects grouping
Checked	flattens everything
🔁 2️⃣ Interleave Across Threads
📌 Meaning

👉 Controls whether rotation is:

per user (thread) OR
shared across all users
🔹 If NOT checked (default)

👉 Each user has its own cycle

Example (3 requests: A, B, C)

| User 1 | A → B → C |
| User 2 | A → B → C |

✔ Independent rotation per thread

🔹 If CHECKED

👉 ALL users share one global sequence

Example:
Step	User	Request
1	User1	A
2	User2	B
3	User1	C
4	User2	A

✔ One global rotation shared

🧪 Real behavior example
Setup:
2 users
3 requests: A, B, C
❌ Interleave Across Threads = OFF
User1: A → B → C
User2: A → B → C

✔ Each user independent

✅ Interleave Across Threads = ON
A (User1)
B (User2)
C (User1)
A (User2)

✔ Global round-robin


🎲 What is Random Controller?

👉 Random Controller selects child requests randomly every time the test runs.

🧠 Simple idea

If you have:

Request A
Request B
Request C

👉 Random Controller will execute like:

Iteration	Output
1	B
2	C
3	A
4	B
5	A

✔ No fixed order

⚙️ How to use Random Controller
Structure:
Thread Group
  └── Random Controller
        ├── Request A
        ├── Request B
        ├── Request C
📋 Fields in Random Controller

👉 Important point:
Random Controller has NO configuration fields.

✔ No dropdown
✔ No checkbox
✔ No numeric input

👉 It is behavior-only controller

🔁 How it behaves
🔹 Case 1: Simple Random Controller
Each iteration:
JMeter picks ONE random child sampler
Executes it
Example (A, B, C):
Iteration	Selected
1	B
2	A
3	C
4	C
5	B
🔹 Case 2: Multiple loops

If loop count = 10:

👉 JMeter randomly selects requests each time
👉 Same request may repeat multiple times
👉 Some requests may not run at all

🔹 Case 3: Random with multiple users

If 100 users:

👉 Each user behaves independently:

User1: random sequence
User2: different random sequence
🧠 Key behavior points

✔ Executes random child each iteration
✔ Does NOT follow order
✔ May repeat same request multiple times
✔ May skip some requests completely

Random Controller Field
🔹 Ignore Sub-Controller Blocks (checkbox)
🧠 What it means

This option controls whether JMeter should:

Treat sub-controllers as single blocks, OR
Break them into individual samplers
⚙️ Behavior
🔹 If UNCHECKED (default)

👉 JMeter treats a sub-controller as one single unit

Example:
Random Controller
   ├── Request A
   ├── Simple Controller
   │       ├── Request B
   │       └── Request C

👉 Behavior:

A, B+C treated as separate blocks
Random selection happens between:
A
(B+C together)

✔ So B and C always run together

🔹 If CHECKED

👉 JMeter ignores grouping inside controllers

Same structure becomes:
A
B
C

👉 Now Random Controller treats everything as:

A OR B OR C (fully flat)

✔ Each request is independent

📊 Comparison
Setting	Behavior
Unchecked	Sub-controller = single block
Checked	All requests treated individually
🧪 Simple example
Structure:
Random Controller
   ├── Login
   ├── Search (Simple Controller)
   │       ├── API1
   │       └── API2
❌ Ignore Sub-Controller Blocks = OFF

Random choices:

Login
(Search group → API1 + API2 together)
✅ Ignore Sub-Controller Blocks = ON

Random choices:

Login
API1
API2
🧠 Easy way to remember
❌ OFF → “Respect grouping”
✅ ON → “Flatten everything”
🎯 Why this matters

Because it changes your test realism:

OFF:

✔ Simulates business flows (grouped actions)

ON:

✔ Simulates independent API calls

🚀 One-line interview answer

In JMeter Random Controller, “Ignore Sub-Controller Blocks” determines whether nested controllers are treated as single execution units or flattened so that individual samplers inside them are randomly executed independently.


🎲 What is Random Order Controller?

👉 It runs all requests inside it, but in a different random order each time.

🧠 Simple idea

If you have:

Request A
Request B
Request C

👉 Random Order Controller will execute:

Iteration 1:
B → A → C
Iteration 2:
C → B → A
Iteration 3:
A → C → B

✔ All run, but order changes every time

⚙️ How to use Random Order Controller
Structure:
Thread Group
  └── Random Order Controller
        ├── Request A
        ├── Request B
        ├── Request C
📋 Fields in Random Order Controller

👉 Important point:

✔ It has NO configurable fields
✔ No dropdowns
✔ No checkboxes

👉 It is purely behavior-based

🔁 How it behaves
🔹 Key rule:

Executes ALL child samplers once per loop, but in random order

🧪 Example
Loop 1:
C
A
B
Loop 2:
B
C
A
Loop 3:
A
B
C
📊 Key behavior points

✔ Executes ALL requests
✔ Randomizes execution order
✔ One full cycle per iteration
✔ No skipping of samplers
✔ No repetition within same loop

🆚 Random Controller vs Random Order Controller
Feature	Random Controller	Random Order Controller
Execution	1 random request	all requests
Order	random pick	shuffled order
Skip samplers	yes	no
Repetition per loop	possible	no


📦 What is Simple Controller?

👉 A Simple Controller just groups multiple samplers together so you can manage them easily.

🧠 Simple idea

Think of it like a folder:

It holds requests
It does NOT control execution behavior
⚙️ How to use Simple Controller
📦 Structure
Thread Group
   ├── Simple Controller (Login Flow)
   │       ├── Login API
   │       └── Get Profile API
   ├── Simple Controller (Search Flow)
   │       ├── Search API
   │       └── View Product API
🔁 How it behaves

👉 Simple Controller does NOT:

randomize
loop
skip
limit

✔ It simply executes its children in order

🧪 Example
Simple Controller
   ├── A
   ├── B
   ├── C
Execution:

👉 A → B → C

✔ Always sequential
✔ Always all executed

📊 Key behavior points

✔ Executes all child requests
✔ Executes in same order
✔ No logic applied
✔ Only for grouping

🎯 Why use Simple Controller?
1️⃣ Organization

👉 Makes test plan clean and readable

2️⃣ Grouping flows

Example:

Login flow
Checkout flow
Search flow
3️⃣ Apply other controllers easily

You can wrap Simple Controller inside:

Loop Controller
Throughput Controller
If Controller
🧪 Example use case
Loop Controller (Loop = 2)
   └── Simple Controller (Checkout Flow)
          ├── Add to Cart
          ├── Payment
          └── Order Confirm

👉 Whole flow repeats 2 times

⚠️ Important point

❌ Simple Controller does NOT control execution
✔ It only groups elements


What is Module Controller?

👉 It allows you to pick and execute another controller (or flow) from somewhere else in the Test Plan

🧠 Simple idea

Think of it like:

🔁 “Shortcut / reference to an existing flow”

Instead of copying the same requests again and again, you just reference them

⚙️ How to use Module Controller
Step 1: Create a reusable flow
Test Plan
   └── Simple Controller (Login Flow)
          ├── Login API
          ├── Get Profile API
Step 2: Use Module Controller
Thread Group
   └── Module Controller → (points to Login Flow)

👉 Now when test runs:
✔ Module Controller will execute Login Flow

📋 Field in Module Controller
🔹 Module To Run

👉 This is a dropdown/tree where you select:

Any controller
Any part of test plan
🔁 How it behaves

👉 When execution reaches Module Controller:

It goes to the selected module
Executes all elements inside it
Then comes back
🧪 Example
Structure:
Simple Controller (Login Flow)
   ├── Login
   └── Profile

Thread Group
   ├── Module Controller → Login Flow
   ├── Search API
Execution:
Login
Profile
Search API
📊 Key behavior points

✔ Reuses existing test flow
✔ Avoids duplication
✔ Executes referenced module fully
✔ Maintains original order

🎯 Why use Module Controller?
1️⃣ Reusability

Same flow used multiple times

2️⃣ Maintainability

Change in one place → reflects everywhere

3️⃣ Cleaner test plan

No repeated code

🧪 Real-world example

Login flow used in multiple scenarios:

Login Flow (defined once)

Thread Group
   ├── Module Controller → Login Flow
   ├── Search

   ├── Module Controller → Login Flow
   ├── Checkout

👉 Login reused without duplication

⚠️ Important points

❌ It does NOT copy requests
✔ It references original structure

❌ Cannot modify inside Module Controller
✔ Changes must be done in original module


Include Controller is used to import and run another JMeter test plan (.jmx file) inside your current test plan.

📥 What is Include Controller?

👉 It allows you to reuse an external .jmx file by including it into your current test.

🧠 Simple idea

Think of it like:

📄 “Insert another JMeter file into this one”

⚙️ How to use Include Controller
✅ Step 1: Create a reusable JMX file

Example: login.jmx

Test Plan
   └── Simple Controller (Login Flow)
          ├── Login API
          └── Get Profile API
✅ Step 2: Use Include Controller in main test
Test Plan
   └── Thread Group
         ├── Include Controller → login.jmx
         ├── Search API
🔁 Execution

👉 When test runs:

Login API
Get Profile API
Search API

✔ Included file executes as part of main test

📋 Field in Include Controller
🔹 Include Controller → Filename

👉 You provide:

Path to .jmx file

Example:

login.jmx

or full path:

C:\tests\login.jmx
🧠 How it behaves

👉 At runtime:

JMeter loads the external JMX
Inserts its content
Executes it as part of current flow
📊 Key behavior points

✔ Reuses external test plans
✔ Avoids duplication
✔ Executes included file fully
✔ Works like copy-paste at runtime

⚠️ Important rules
1️⃣ File must exist

❌ Wrong path → test fails

2️⃣ Path handling

✔ Keep file in same folder (best practice)
✔ Or use relative path

3️⃣ Variables & data

👉 Included file shares:

variables
context
thread execution
🧪 Real-world use case
Example:

You have separate modules:

login.jmx
search.jmx
checkout.jmx

Main test:

Thread Group
   ├── Include → login.jmx
   ├── Include → search.jmx
   ├── Include → checkout.jmx

✔ Clean modular design


